<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Move Around – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Let's Dance!</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Move Around</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: productive: Recite simple nursery rhymes. interactive: Engage with simple commands using basic words and gestures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can recite simple action rhymes (e.g., 'head, shoulders, knees, and toes'). Can give very simple commands to peers (e.g., says 'head,' point to head). Can participate in conversations about movements, giving simple commands (e.g., 'shake' or 'turn').</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts with the familiar action rhyme "Head, Shoulders, Knees, and Toes" to activate prior knowledge of body parts and movement vocabulary. Students stand in a circle and follow the teacher's actions and singing. The teacher emphasizes clear pronunciation of target sounds /t/ and /d/ during the song (e.g., "toes," "dance").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models all actions and words, ensuring slow and clear pronunciation. For example, the teacher says "Jump!" and jumps, then "Shake!" and shakes their body. The teacher also uses flashcards with action verbs (e.g., "dance," "spin," "turn") while performing the action. For phonemic awareness, the teacher points to the letter 'J' on a flashcard while saying "Jump!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses TPR and visual aids to check understanding of key verbs and prepositions.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Show me 'jump'!" (Students jump.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is this 'slow' or 'fast'?" (Teacher demonstrates a slow then fast movement, students respond chorally.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Where is 'next to'?" (Teacher stands next to a chair, students point or imitate.)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date]<br>[Time]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Let's Dance!")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large illustrated sequence of simple dance commands (e.g., "First, one step forward," "Next, spin around," "Jump over the line," "Stand next to the chair"). These are presented as big, colorful flashcards or a projected sequence.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Engage with simple commands using basic words and gestures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows a sequence of 3-4 illustrated dance commands. Each card shows an action (e.g., 'First, take one step to the side.' 'Next, spin around.' 'Jump over the line.')</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher models each command with the corresponding action, speaking slowly and clearly, emphasizing sequential commands ("First," "Next") and prepositions ("to the side," "over," "next to"). The teacher explicitly pronounces /t/ in 'two' and /d/ in 'dance' when these words appear.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students chorally repeat the commands after the teacher, performing the actions (TPR).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Follow the Picture:</b> The teacher shuffles the command cards and says a command (e.g., "Jump over the line!"). Students point to the correct picture and perform the action.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Show Me the Move:</b> The teacher says a command, and students perform the action immediately. (e.g., "Stand next to the chair!", "Move to the front!").</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date]<br>[Time]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice sequential commands with numbers (e.g., 'First, take one step to the side.' 'Next, take two steps to the side.'), simple directive phrases (e.g., 'Move to the front.' 'Step to the side.'), and positional commands with prepositions (e.g., 'Stand next to the chair.' 'Jump over the line.') using vocabulary: move, dance, step, spin, turn, twirl, shake, hop, put, jump, one to ten, in, out, around, up, next to, over.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Choral Command Repetition (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher displays flashcards with actions and associated commands (e.g., "One step!", "Jump up!"). Students chorally repeat the commands while performing the actions. The teacher ensures correct pronunciation of /t/ and /d/ and focuses on intonation. The teacher also introduces letter sounds for S, T, U, V by showing flashcards and having students identify words that start with these sounds (e.g., 'S' for 'spin', 'T' for 'turn').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Teacher-Led Command Chain (Semi-controlled):</b> The teacher starts a command chain: "First, spin!" (students spin and repeat). Then the teacher adds: "Next, jump!" (students perform both actions and repeat "First, spin! Next, jump!"). The teacher continues, adding new commands like "Take one step to the side," "Move around the circle." This builds memory and sequential understanding.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Command Dice Game (Pair Interaction/Peer Check):</b> In pairs, students take turns rolling a large soft dice with pictures of actions (e.g., 'shake', 'turn', 'hop', 'step', 'jump', 'twirl'). When a student rolls an action, they say the command (e.g., "Shake!") and perform it. Their partner then says "Good job! Now, my turn." The teacher encourages the use of polite expressions like 'after you' and 'go ahead' when students swap turns.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date]<br>[Time]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Let's Dance!")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative "Dance Leader" Activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are divided into small groups (3-4 children). Each group receives a set of picture cards representing different movements (e.g., 'one step', 'spin', 'jump over the line', 'stand next to the chair', 'shake fast', 'move slow').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Within each group, students take turns being the "Dance Leader." The leader chooses one or two cards and gives simple commands to their group (e.g., "First, spin!", "Next, two steps!"). The other group members follow the commands.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, providing support and encouraging the use of polite turns ("After you," "Go ahead"). The activity focuses on students initiating and responding to commands related to movements and dance. The teacher prompts students to use sequential numbers and prepositions.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The activity concludes with each group demonstrating a short sequence of their dance commands for the class, with the leader speaking the commands.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date]<br>[Time]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Dance Leader" activity and final group presentations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student, as a "Dance Leader," gives at least two simple movement commands to their small group or the whole class (e.g., "Shake!", "Jump up!"). Other students demonstrate understanding by following the commands.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of key verbs and sounds (/t/, /d/, 'J' in 'jump').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., 'shake', 'turn', 'step', 'jump', 'next to').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of commands and appropriate gestures.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active participation and collaboration in group activities.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Following sequential commands accurately.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students draw their favorite move from the lesson and show it to the teacher. They also point to a happy face or a neutral face to show how they feel about giving commands.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can say 'jump' and jump!" (Yes/No with thumbs up/down)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I like to dance and move." (Happy/Sad face drawing)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date]<br>[Time]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite dance move today?" (Students can say the word or show the action).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What command did you say to your friends?" (Students recall and articulate a simple command).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this speaking lesson? The TPR activities and the "Dance Leader" game generated high engagement and oral output. Choral repetition helped build confidence for individual command giving. Students showed good understanding of 'shake', 'jump', 'step', and 'next to'.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? Some students still hesitated with sequential commands ("First, Next"). More explicit modeling and practice with numbered sequences will be beneficial. Also, some needed more prompting for polite expressions. For Lesson 4 (Writing), I will integrate drawing and labeling their favorite dance moves and simple command phrases to reinforce the vocabulary learned today.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This speaking lesson focused on actively using movement commands. The next lesson (Writing) will build upon this by having students draw and label their favorite dance moves and simple commands, reinforcing the vocabulary and concepts visually and in written form.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date]<br>[Time]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>